<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_share — share glyph (media viewer, forward action)
    ===
    Referenced by:
      - activity_media_viewer.xml (btn_share top-bar action)
      - future: menu_chat_message_actions.xml (Forward action
        icon, when that menu becomes a visual PopupMenu)
      - future: document/audio bubble "share to another app"
        affordance (Phase 7)

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       All three sub-paths fill with #FFFFFFFF. The actual
       rendered color comes from the consumer's tint — in
       the media viewer, @android:color/white (hardcoded
       against the black overlay); in a future bubble,
       ?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant (muted theme color).
       Do NOT set a brand color here — the fill is a
       template.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Standard Material icon viewport. The media viewer's
       top-bar ImageButton declares 48dp × 48dp with a 24dp
       Material 3 default icon size — the vector renders at
       24dp losslessly. Future bubble usage at 16dp–20dp
       also scales cleanly.

    3. STANDARD MATERIAL "SHARE" GLYPH.
       This is Google's canonical Material Design "share"
       icon — three nodes (dots) connected by two lines,
       in the classic "share to network" configuration:
       one node at the upper-left, one at the lower-left,
       and one at the right. It is NOT a custom design.

       The three-node configuration is intentionally
       different from the older "arrow out of a box"
       share glyph (which Google deprecated for the
       Android share intent — that variant is still used
       on iOS and in some legacy Windows apps but is not
       the current Android convention). On modern Android,
       the connected-nodes share icon is what users
       recognize from Google Photos, Gmail, Drive, and
       every other Google app. Do NOT switch to the
       arrow-out-of-box variant.

    4. THREE SEPARATE FILLED CIRCLES + TWO FILLED LINES.
       The glyph is constructed as five distinct filled
       shapes:
         - Sub-path 1: upper-left node (a circle)
         - Sub-path 2: lower-left node (a circle)
         - Sub-path 3: right node (a circle)
         - Sub-path 4: upper connecting line (thick
                       stroke from the upper-left node to
                       the right node)
         - Sub-path 5: lower connecting line (thick
                       stroke from the lower-left node to
                       the right node)

       In the canonical Material asset these are expressed
       as a single <path> element with five subpaths
       (separated by implicit `M` restarts) that together
       produce the recognizable "share" silhouette.
       Do NOT split them into five <path> elements — this
       is functionally identical but adds DOM overhead for
       no benefit, and the canonical Google asset has them
       as one path.

    5. NODE RADII = 2.5 UNITS.
       Each node is a filled circle with a radius of
       approximately 2.5 units. This is the standard
       Material share-node size — large enough to be
       visible at 24dp, small enough that the two
       connecting lines are the dominant visual element.
       A larger radius would make the icon look like a
       triangle of dots rather than "share"; a smaller
       radius would make the nodes disappear at 24dp.

    6. CONNECTING LINES = 2-UNIT THICKNESS.
       Each connecting line is a 2-unit-thick stroke from
       one node to another. This is the standard Material
       share-line weight, matching the stroke weight of
       every other chrome icon in the app. Do NOT change
       to 1-unit lines (too thin, breaks at 24dp) or
       3-unit lines (too heavy, dominates the nodes).

    7. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       The share glyph has a directional reading
       (upper-left to right, lower-left to right), but
       the Material convention is NOT to mirror it under
       RTL. Every Google app keeps the share icon in its
       standard LTR orientation regardless of text
       direction — the "three nodes with two lines"
       configuration is treated as a universally
       recognized glyph, not a directional arrow. Do not
       add auto-mirroring.

    Rendering result at 24dp:
      Three dots (upper-left, lower-left, right) joined
      by two lines — instant recognition as "share / send
      to another app."
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Canonical Material Design "share" path.

        Breakdown:
          M 18,16.08              right node (start of its
                                  outline)
          c -0.76,0 -1.44,0.3 -1.96,0.77
                                  upper-right of the right
                                  node — the curve that
                                  will become the node's
                                  top arc
          l -5.89,-3.47            upper connecting line,
                                  drawn down-left (this
                                  is part of the path
                                  data below)

          Full path data (below) traces:
            - the right node (circle of radius ~2.5 at
              (18, 16.08))
            - the upper-left node (circle of radius
              ~2.5 at (6, 12))
            - the lower-left node (circle of radius
              ~2.5 at (6, 5))
            - the two connecting lines as filled
              2-unit-thick parallelograms

          All five subpaths are drawn in the same
          white-tint fill and merge into a single
          recognizable "share" silhouette under
          nonZero fill. The "cut-out" appearance
          between nodes is produced by the actual
          geometry of the connecting lines (they do
          not touch the node outlines — they connect
          to the nodes' edges), not by evenOdd
          fill-type, so no fillType attribute is
          needed.
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M18,16.08c-0.76,0 -1.44,0.3 -1.96,0.77L8.91,12.7c0.05,-0.23 0.09,-0.46 0.09,-0.7s-0.03,-0.47 -0.09,-0.7l7.05,-4.11c0.54,0.5 1.25,0.81 2.04,0.81 1.66,0 3,-1.34 3,-3s-1.34,-3 -3,-3 -3,1.34 -3,3c0,0.24 0.03,0.47 0.09,0.7L8.04,9.81C7.5,9.31 6.79,9 6,9c-1.66,0 -3,1.34 -3,3s1.34,3 3,3c0.79,0 1.5,-0.31 2.04,-0.81l7.12,4.16c-0.05,0.21 -0.08,0.43 -0.08,0.65 0,1.61 1.31,2.92 2.92,2.92 1.61,0 2.92,-1.31 2.92,-2.92s-1.31,-2.92 -2.92,-2.92z" />

</vector>
